AB  - Agentic AI systems represent a fundamental shift in the relationship between Artificial intelligence and organizational action. Unlike their predecessors, these systems do not merely execute predefined processes or generate isolated outputs. Instead, they perceive, plan, act, remember, and collaborate in ways that are qualitatively closer to organizational actors than to conventional software tools. This shift demands a profound transformation in governance: one that operates jointly at the technical, organizational, and regulatory levels, and that evolves dynamically as agentic systems grow in capability and deployment scope. However, the major governance frameworks were introduced before the emergence of Agentic AI and failed to cope with agentic-specific requirements. This paper introduces a three-layer governance framework spanning technical orchestration, organisational integration, and regulatory alignment, operationalized through a five-level maturity model and illustrated through a supply chain use case that exercises all three layers simultaneously. A central finding is that governance maturity is bounded by the weakest layer: technical investment without corresponding organizational authority structures or regulatory alignment does not constitute genuine governance.
