AB  - Modern vulnerability management suffers from a critical “context gap”: generic vulnerability records, such as CVEs, describe technical flaws but lack the organizational specifics needed to assess real-world risk. Traditional prioritization methods relying solely on CVSS scores frequently misalign remediation efforts with actual business impact, leading to wasted resources and unaddressed critical exposures. This paper introduces a context-aware vulnerability intelligence framework that bridges Application Risk Profiles (ARPs)—structured representations of an organization’s technology stack, data sensitivity, threat landscape, and security controls—with CVE knowledge. Our system operates bidirectionally: (1) recommending relevant vulnerabilities for a given application profile, and (2) rapidly identifying affected applications when new threats emerge. The framework employs semantic retrieval to find semantically similar application profiles and evidence-grounded reasoning to generate actionable, explainable recommendations—all within a privacy-preserving, locally hosted architecture. Experimental evaluation on 1000 structured ARPs achieves an F1-score of 94.08% for vulnerability recommendation and strong retrieval performance for impact analysis. By anchoring automated reasoning in retrieved ARP evidence, the system provides security teams and executives with prioritized, context-aware insights that align technical remediation with business risk.
