AB  - Autonomous vehicles (AVs) have great potential for increased safety, efficiency, and accessibility. However, achieving full autonomy is hindered by ongoing safety and security challenges. Although there have been improvements, AI in AVs continues to struggle with edge cases, adversarial and other types of attacks, and the availability of labeled datasets to train on. This article reviews the Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) as a self-supervised method to overcome challenges associated with AVs. JEPA, unlike traditional pixel reconstruction and explicit pair models, makes use of joint embedding spaces that allows for improved efficiency in computation and semantic understanding while being less reliant on labeled datasets. JEPA provides extra safety features through the predictive ability, better perception in poor conditions, and anomaly detection; and offers additional security with adversarial robustness, intrusion detection, and privacy protection. The review looks at variations of JEPA including I-JEPA for images, V-JEPA for videos, and AD-L-JEPA for driving with LIDAR: where unsolved issues consist of sensor fusion, modeling temporal components, real time decision-making, and predicting failure. JEPA-type methods have been demonstrated as more interpretable and computationally efficient when compared to traditional approaches. This review is meant to provoke future research into incorporating JEPA into AVs, focusing on the need for multi-modal architectures, real time constraints, and compliance, suggesting a host of possibilities for use cases in improving AV safety and security using the predictive modeling offered by JEPA.
ER  - 

