AB  - Financial services increasingly rely on artificial intelligence (AI) and, in particular, on generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) systems for high-stakes managerial and decision processes. However, existing deployments often remain aspirational: responsible artificial intelligence (RAI) principles are articulated at a high level, but operational procedures, evidentiary requirements, and accountability structures are insufficiently specified. This study conducts a systematic literature review (SLR) of 136 studies published between 2021 and 2025, combining peer-reviewed publications with selected preprint and working-paper evidence on generative artificial intelligence in financial services. From the perspective of the artificial intelligence field, the review consolidates fragmented work on models, tasks, and risks, and identifies three recurrent gaps: a translation gap between abstract ethics principles and verifiable practices, a domain-specificity gap concerning fiduciary duty, legal traceability, and systemic risk, and a lifecycle gap in adapting models and processes to evolving data and regulation. The novelty of the study is that it turns this synthesis into a finance-specific operational framework that links the implemented AI technology, namely GenAI systems, with the application domain of auditable financial decision engineering. Building on these insights, the study proposes the Collaborativity–Controllability–Co-evolution (3C-RAI) framework as a cognitive partnership paradigm in which generative artificial intelligence systems provide contestable, evidence-based analysis while human experts retain decision authority. From the perspective of engineering applications and decision and managerial engineering in financial services, the framework is further developed into an operational design for human and artificial intelligence collaboration. Collaborativity structures evidence-first interaction patterns and role division in customer-facing, operational, and decision-augmentation workflows. Controllability specifies governance mechanisms, documentation, software components, and intervention points that make responsibility traceable for managers, risk officers, and regulators. Co-evolution introduces trigger-based reassessment, monitoring, and re-approval procedures to keep deployments aligned with changing data, rules, and business conditions. The paper also provides evaluation indicators and a reference implementation architecture. Together, these contributions link advances in generative artificial intelligence to auditable, resilient, and human-centered decision processes in financial institutions.
