KW  - Agentic AI
KW  - Governance Framework
KW  - Predictive Maintenance
KW  - Digital Twin
KW  - Multi-Agent System
KW  - Semantic Communications
KW  - Knowledge Graph
AB  - As agentic AI systems are deployed in safety-critical manufacturing environments, traditional hierarchical governance structures prove inadequate for managing autonomous decision-making at machine speed. This paper introduces the concept of the Governance Twin. This framework represents a distributed multi-agent architecture that enables co-evolutionary governance of digital twin-enabled predictive maintenance systems. The framework implements a Sentinel+Council structure where sentinel agents continuously monitor operational agents for emergent vulnerabilities, behavioral drift, and constraint violations, while council agents negotiate responses that balance competing objectives through collaborative deliberation rather than hierarchical control. Unlike classic software agents that rely on syntactic communication through predefined programming constructs, agentic AI leverages semantic communication to dynamically interpret intent and select appropriate tools, models, and actions not explicitly programmed at development time. We demonstrate how this approach manages inherent tensions between operational goals and intrinsic safety requirements—uptime, efficiency, and profitability—through contextual adaptive decision-making and structural interdependence between AI operations and human safety values. Integration with knowledge graphs and other tools enables causal reasoning and explainable decision-making while maintaining governance at machine speed. The framework is instantiated within predictive maintenance scenarios to illustrate vulnerability detection, multi-objective negotiation, and prevention of cascading failures in industrial systems. © 2026 The Authors. Published by ELSEVIER Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0) Peer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the NAMRI|SME.
ER  - 

