AB  - In the recent decade, as artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) have been increasingly employed across various sectors, ranging from healthcare to critical national infrastructures, it has become an increasingly attractive target for adversaries, highlighting the urgency of studying AI/ML security. Hence, the current study provides a review and bibliometric analysis of the literature on adversarial attacks and countermeasures within the AI/ML security field. Our methodology involves utilizing the Scopus API to identify the most relevant articles from 2015 to 2024, a preprocessing step, followed by the visualization of authors' keywords using the VOSviewer tool, and statistical analysis. The network visualizations allowed a detailed understanding of the interconnections and thematic progressions within the AI/ML security domain. Additionally, through the statistical analysis, we performed the keywords' frequency analysis along with examining their growth rates and P-values to assess the prominence and evolution of various research themes over time. Finally, we present two taxonomies of ML attacks and their corresponding countermeasures derived from the bibliometric analysis and examination of the collected studies. These taxonomies highlight a dynamic interplay between increasingly complex adversarial tactics, including poisoning, backdoor, evasion, model inversion, and membership inference attacks, and the sophisticated defensive responses developed to counter them, such as data sanitization, adversarial training, model hardening, differential privacy, and homomorphic encryption. This study underscores the efforts made to enhance the security frameworks of AI/ML systems against evolving threats, aiming to inspire further research and strengthen cybersecurity measures in advanced ML deployments.
ER  - 

