AB  - As the digital transformation of railways makes heterogeneous data assets critical for safety and efficiency, a critical security gap has emerged. Traditional cybersecurity frameworks, while essential for perimeter defense, fail to protect assets after legitimate access. This leaves data vulnerable to internal threats, illicit redistribution, and tampering, as these frameworks lack content-bound security guarantees. This paper bridges this gap by linking cutting-edge digital watermarking theory with the specific application needs of the railway sector. Our investigation comprises three primary contributions: (1) We establish a full-lifecycle risk profile for railway digital assets; (2) We introduce an application-oriented decision framework to evaluate watermarking paradigms across three core asset categories: perceptual data, structured databases, and AI-driven models; (3) We identify critical open challenges and outline a future research agenda in this interdisciplinary research. This review serves as a foundational guide to this emerging field, aiming to build the essential trust foundations for next-generation intelligent transportation systems.
