AB  - The rapid deployment of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) in pattern recognition tasks necessitates a rigorous assessment of model trustworthiness, particularly concerning multimodal ethical alignment. While LVLMs demonstrate remarkable efficacy in standard vision-language tasks, their security boundaries regarding ethical nuances in complex, context-dependent scenarios remain largely unquantified. Discrepancies between visual perception and textual reasoning pose substantial risks to the deployment of Responsible AI, especially in safety–critical domains. To address this gap and systematically interrogate the safety limitations of contemporary foundation models, we introduce VALUE-Bench, a hierarchical evaluation framework designed to benchmark LVLM ethical robustness. Focusing on the construction of robust security metrics, VALUE-Bench employs a four-pillar assessment protocol: (1) Ethical Understanding Accuracy, (2) Adversarial Robustness, (3) Reasoning Reliability, and (4) Resistance to Misuse. We curated a comprehensive suite of six datasets spanning ten high-stakes ethical tasks, including hate speech detection and implicit stereotype mitigation. Our extensive evaluation of 19 state-of-the-art LVLMs reveals significant disparities in cross-modal alignment and exposes critical vulnerabilities in current representation learning mechanisms. VALUE-Bench serves as both a diagnostic tool and a foundational benchmark for enhancing the security and trustworthiness of next-generation multimodal AI. We release our benchmark and code at https://github.com/fenffef/VALUE-Bench. Warning: This paper contains harmful content to illustrate security vulnerabilities.
