AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enables phishing detection systems to incorporate external cybersecurity knowledge during inference. However, reliance on retrieved evidence introduces a distinct robustness problem: relevant cybersecurity information may be accompanied by semantically plausible but irrelevant documents that can interfere with the model’s decision. We refer to this phenomenon as retrieval contamination. Existing phishing detection studies primarily evaluate robustness to perturbations of the input message, while conventional RAG and generic retrieval-aware fine-tuning do not explicitly investigate cybersecurity-specific evidence discrimination under controlled retrieval contamination. We formulate retrieval contamination as an explicit robustness dimension of retrieval-augmented phishing detection and propose RAFT-Phish, a retrieval-aware fine-tuning framework that jointly learns phishing classification, evidence discrimination, and structured evidence-grounded rationale generation. The framework combines hard-negative retrieval supervision with structured cyber threat intelligence (CTI) and MITRE ATT&CK grounding. We evaluate RAFT-Phish on a hybrid benchmark containing legitimate enterprise communications, human-written phishing, and Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI)-generated phishing, together with controlled retrieval contamination, adversarial input perturbations, cross-domain evaluation, and external phishing datasets. On the main test set, RAFT-Phish achieves an accuracy of 0.92 and an Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) of 0.95. Under increasing retrieval contamination, RAFT-Phish maintains higher accuracy than standard RAG and remains competitive with generic RAFT. Ablation experiments further indicate that cybersecurity grounding, distractor-aware supervision, and structured rationale generation contribute to the observed performance on the evaluated split. These findings motivate retrieval robustness as an explicit evaluation dimension for LLM-based phishing detection systems that rely on external cybersecurity knowledge.
ER  - 

