AB  - With the advent of data-driven artificial intelligence (AI), particularly embodied AI, human–AI bidirectional interaction has rapidly expanded across a wide range of digital and intelligent scenarios. In this context, data has become the primary medium for cross-system risk propagation. Once input data is compromised, risks may be amplified along business processes and control loops, thereby endangering both the system and its operational environment. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a security architecture for human–AI interaction, structured around “data flows” as the central thread. The architecture employs scenario modelling to construct a reference meta-model, mapping security controls across six perspectives: performance, business, data, algorithm, application, and infrastructure. Building on this scenario modelling approach, we design a reference meta-model and apply it to the industrial case of aluminium electrolysis. By developing an industrial monitoring system, we explore both static and dynamic protective measures against the anode effect. In doing so, we not only validate the effectiveness of the proposed architecture but also aim to provide a security solution tailored to industrial scenarios.
ER  - 

