AB  - The accelerating growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) has reshaped modern digital infrastructure while dramatically widening the attack surface of distributed, resource-constrained, and heterogeneous environments. Conventional intrusion detection systems (IDS), designed for traditional IT settings, no longer meet the demands of smart cities, industrial automation, connected vehicles, and medical systems, where energy budgets, latency bounds, and operational continuity are as decisive as detection accuracy. This chapter charts the future of sustainable IoT security and argues for a deliberate shift away from accuracy-centric evaluation toward architectures that are deployment-aware, energy-conscious, and secure across the full system lifecycle. It examines how artificial intelligence, such as machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning, federated learning, and neurosymbolic models, has become central to adaptive threat detection, while confronting the practical constraints of compression, edge deployment, adversarial robustness, interpretability, and dataset realism. The discussion then turns to blockchain as a coordination and provenance layer for decentralized trust, to post-quantum cryptographic approaches that safeguard long-term integrity against quantum-capable adversaries, and to hierarchical edge-fog-cloud strategies that reconcile detection strength with sustainability. Domain-specific analyses of smart cities, transportation and V2X systems, the Internet of Medical Things, and industrial control systems illustrate divergent deployment realities. Finally, the chapter addresses the governance, standardization, and policy mechanisms, including energy-aware benchmarking and certification, required to translate research advances into resilient, scalable, and genuinely green IoT intrusion detection.
