AB  - Digital twins have emerged as a pivotal technology in modern power and energy systems to improve performance, reduce maintenance costs, and ensure safer operation. Today’s energy infrastructure faces critical challenges regarding stability, control, cyber-physical vulnerabilities, and multi-carrier complexities. However, existing literature remains fragmented across isolated domains, lacking a unified reference architecture across the energy value chain. To address this gap, this review systematically examines digital twin applications across the evolving energy industry and proposes the DT-Energy 5 L reference framework. Results show that combining machine learning with digital twins significantly enhances capabilities: in production, facilitating plant optimization; in transmission and distribution, improving grid reliability and load management; in consumption and storage, optimizing energy patterns and battery performance; and in markets, enabling Peer-to-Peer trading. Furthermore, in Integrated Energy Systems, digital twins coordinate multi-carrier hubs for real-time scheduling while providing AI-driven active defense mechanisms to ensure cybersecurity resilience. Ultimately, this study provides a strategic roadmap demonstrating that overcoming protocol heterogeneity and deploying unified security standards are critical to realizing resilient, secure, and zero-carbon smart grid transitions.
