AB  - Embodied AI aims to develop intelligent systems with physical embodiments capable of perceiving, decision-making, acting, and learning in real-world environments. Despite decades of exploration, it remains challenging for embodied agents to achieve human-level intelligence for general-purpose tasks in open dynamic environments. Recent breakthroughs in large models and world models have empowered embodied AI by enhancing perception, planning, interaction and learning. In this article, we provide a comprehensive survey on large-model-empowered embodied AI, focusing on autonomous decision-making and embodied learning. We investigate both hierarchical and end-to-end decision-making paradigms, detailing how large models enhance high-level planning, low-level execution, and feedback for hierarchical decision-making, and how vision-language-action models, state-space models, generative action models, and world action models advance end-to-end decision-making. For embodied learning, we review mainstream learning methodologies, elaborating on how large models enhance imitation learning, reinforcement learning, transfer learning and meta-learning. Further, we integrate world models into the survey, presenting their architectures and functional roles in empowering decision-making and learning in synergy with large models. Though solid advances have been achieved, challenges still exist, which are discussed at the end of this survey, highlighting potential future research directions.
ER  - 

