AB  - Cyber attack surfaces are expanding at an unprecedented pace, and modern adversaries are increasingly capable of operating with autonomy. As a result, organizations can no longer rely solely on periodic or manual security assessments, they require offensive security mechanisms that work continuously, adapt in real time, and scale beyond human limitations. Although AI driven threat validation has emerged as a powerful enabler in this direction, the landscape remains fragmented, with substantial gaps in methodological rigor, operational maturity, and real world deployment readiness. This paper provides a comprehensive, multidimensional analysis of how Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Generative AI, and Graph Intelligence are currently being leveraged to automate attack discovery, exploit generation, and exposure validation across modern infrastructures. The literature synthesis indicates that, despite notable progress, many existing approaches still exhibit limitations in areas such as online learning capability, transparent decision logic, secure autonomous behavior, and diversity of simulated attack payloads. These limitations often require significant human involvement for interpretation, oversight, and safe operational control. By consolidating current advancements, identifying cross cutting gaps, and outlining promising research pathways, this study positions AI driven offensive security as a transformative but still developing domain. The insights presented here highlight the urgent need for self improving attack simulation, trustworthy and explainable automation, and ethically grounded continuous threat validation frameworks capable of keeping pace with today’s dynamic threat landscape.
ER  - 

