AB  - The safety of Autonomous Vehicles relies heavily on the robust perception in heterogeneous environments; however, vulnerabilities within both physical sensor mechanisms and deep learning algorithms create a critical bottleneck for real-world deployment. Unlike prior surveys restricted to isolated modalities, this paper presents a comprehensive review that rigorously bridges the gap between single-sensor vulnerabilities and multi-sensor fusion fragility. Spanning Camera, LiDAR, Radar, and GPS modalities, we analyze pivotal studies through a novel Task-Centric Taxonomy. Our analysis follows a bottom-up trajectory: we first dissect the fundamental perturbation mechanisms in individual sensors, and then elucidate how these single-modality attacks exploit fusion logic flaws to precipitate system-wide cascading failures, effectively exposing the redundancy paradox in current fusion architectures. To counteract these threats, we systematically categorize contemporary countermeasures into a hierarchical defense framework consisting of model-level immunity, data-level purification, and system-level cross-modal arbitration. Furthermore, we critique the validity gap in evaluation protocols and posit that emerging paradigms, such as End-to-End driving and World Models, inevitably expand the adversarial attack surface. To foster sustainable research, we maintain a continuously updated repository at https://github.com/Lyon2020/MSP-Attacks.
