AB  - Securing smart manufacturing increasingly requires a resilience-oriented cybersecurity perspective that extends beyond the factory floor to include digitally connected assets, operational dependencies, and supply-chain interfaces. In this paper, smart manufacturing refers to the digitally enabled production environment, resilience refers to the ability of that environment to anticipate, withstand, recover from, and adapt to cyber–physical disruption, and supply chains are treated as an extension of the same risk surface through which trust, traceability, software, components, and operational dependencies propagate. This paper synthesizes peer-reviewed literature and authoritative guidance to characterize threats, defenses, and operational gaps at the system level of manufacturing systems. Using a PRISMA-style protocol, we map evidence across hardware/firmware, software/data pipelines, and AI security; evaluate detection and response mechanisms (digital twins, anomaly/intrusion recognition, zero-trust patterns); and relate these to standards and sector profiles relevant to OT/IT interoperability. Building on the synthesis, we propose a six-pillar framework for cyber–physical resilience: (1) cyber–physical risk engineering design for secure manufacturing; (2) federated testbeds for verifiable trust and secure interoperability; (3) adaptive cyber–physical threat response; (4) risk-informed decision-making and cyber-risk communication; (5) adoption pathways and operational implementation; and (6) embedded cybersecurity culture and engineering literacy. The framework aligns technology, governance, and workforce levers to reduce downtime risk, improve recoverability, and support scalable deployment across heterogeneous assets and supply networks. We conclude with a research agenda that prioritizes traceability and verification, testbed-driven validation, quantification of cyber risk and value-at-risk, lifecycle management standards, and field evaluations, providing actionable steps to advance resilient, efficient, and sustainable manufacturing systems, anchored in verifiable evidence and practical validation.
