AB  - Deploying small-scale inference agents to the edge significantly reduces service latency, but it also makes the effective security guarantee inherently dependent on where inference is hosted and how traffic reaches the host. To protect against such cyber-attacks, especially in security-sensitive applications such as medical diagnostics and autonomous driving, clients must strictly enforce service security requirements regarding the importance of their inference tasks. To meet such requirements, one may implement security-aware network functions (S-NFs) like firewalls and deep packet inspection on edge servers along the forwarding path while maximizing the cost-efficiency of this deployment. However, existing studies typically optimize agent placement or security provisioning in isolation, which becomes fundamentally insufficient when client-specific security requirements must be met through shared and overlapping S-NFs under stringent capacity and routing constraints. This decoupled approach inevitably leads to higher implementation costs and resource over-provisioning. To address this gap, this work introduces an integrated framework for security-aware inference deployment in NFV-enabled edge services. Assuming that the joint allocation of agents and S-NFs can eliminate redundant resource consumption, we formally establish a novel problem called Security-aware Inference Agent Deployment in NFV-enabled networks (SIAD) to optimize the overall implementation cost and prove its NP-hardness. To solve this problem, we design an efficient Cost-Security Efficiency Index (CSEI) measure and develop an innovative CSEI measure-based Security-aware NFV-based Agent Deployment (CS-ND) algorithm, which is mathematically proven to guarantee a logarithm-approximate performance bound within polynomial time complexity. Extensive simulations show that CS-ND outperforms standard benchmarks by an average of 42.16% in overall implementation cost, 39.70% in cost per security level, and up to 44.19% in the average client-to-node hop count under the adopted hop-based routing-cost model. Furthermore, evaluations against advanced baselines demonstrate that CS-ND accurately approximates the global optimum with a narrow 6.68% mean optimality gap against an exact solver (Exact-ND), while achieving a far superior cost-runtime trade-off compared to a Simulated Annealing metaheuristic (SA-ND) that incurs a 16.5× execution delay for marginal cost gains.
