AB  - Blockchain-assisted federated learning has emerged as a promising paradigm for privacy-preserving collaborative training across IoT devices. However, most existing frameworks treat malicious behavior detection, trust evaluation, and economic incentives as independent modules, making it difficult to establish clear causal links between a participant’s actions and the resulting system-wide consequences. To address this limitation, this paper presents BRITE-FL, a federated learning framework that unifies behavioral evidence, reputation dynamics, and token-based settlement into a single closed-loop workflow governed by on-chain smart contracts. The framework separates heterogeneous IoT devices into training and validation roles, where validators independently score each submitted update and reach detection consensus through a majority-based protocol. The detection outcome is fed into a reputation mechanism with exponentially escalating penalties, which in turn determines each node’s deposit requirement, reward share, and continued eligibility, forming a self-reinforcing closed loop between behavioral evidence and economic consequences. Experiments on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST under data poisoning and label flipping attacks with up to 30% malicious participants show that BRITE-FL limits model accuracy degradation to within 5% and achieves a detection rate exceeding 94%, outperforming four baseline schemes. Blockchain integration introduces less than 9% overhead per training round, with median transaction confirmation delay below 0.7 s at scales up to 1000 nodes. These results demonstrate that tightly coupling behavioral evidence with on-chain reputation and economic enforcement provides an effective and scalable approach to securing federated learning in heterogeneous IoT environments.
