AB  - This paper presents a systematic survey of the privacy–utility trade-offs in Federated Learning (FL), organized around a novel defense–protection–verification (DPV) taxonomy. Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training while preserving data privacy through decentralized parameter exchange, yet its deployment is fundamentally governed by the tension between privacy protection intensity and model utility. This trade-off is most acute in the Internet of Things (IoT), where billions of resource-constrained edge devices operate over bandwidth-scarce wireless links, rendering the choice of privacy-preserving mechanisms subordinate to stringent communication constraints. We present a systematic survey of FL’s privacy–utility landscape, organized around a novel multi-layered defense–protection–verification technical framework. We comparatively analyze foundational privacy-preserving mechanisms, differential privacy (DP), homomorphic encryption (HE), secure multi-party computation (SMPC), trusted execution environments (TEE), and blockchain with zero-knowledge proofs, evaluating their distinct privacy-utility profiles alongside their communication overhead implications: DP introduces lightweight per-round transmission but prolongs convergence; HE ensures near-zero exposure risk yet imposes catastrophic ciphertext expansion reaching two orders of magnitude; SMPC enables trustless collaboration but scales linearly in communication with model dimension. We further dissect three core FL architectures (horizontal, vertical, and federated transfer learning) and contextualize their trade-off characteristics through cross-domain empirical analysis, foregrounding IoT as the paradigmatic setting where communication constraints most sharply shape the privacy-utility frontier, alongside healthcare and financial risk control where network conditions permit alternative balancing strategies. We identify critical future directions including communication-layer co-design for bandwidth-limited deployments, multi-objective optimization theories that treat communication efficiency as integral to the privacy-utility design space, and lightweight adaptive mechanisms for resource-constrained environments. This work provides contextualized implementation guidelines to advance FL from technical feasibility toward operational excellence in privacy-sensitive, communication-limited domains.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - Responses of AI chatbots to escalating suicide risk: A simulation study of repeated interactions
AU  - Pichowicz, Wojciech
AU  - Kotas, Marek
AU  - Kalka, Natalia
AU  - Dembowska, Julia
AU  - Karska, Julia
AU  - Piotrowski, Patryk
