KW  - Multi-modal data
KW  - Concept drift
KW  - Security applications
KW  - Machine learning
AB  - Concept drift poses a persistent challenge for machine learning models deployed in security applications, where evolving attacks, limited labels, and the diversification of data modalities make robust adaptation particularly difficult. Existing surveys mainly emphasize general-purpose or unimodal settings and do not provide a unified, security-oriented account of how drift emerges, propagates, and should be handled across structured, textual, visual, and multimodal data. To address this gap, this survey proposes a unified cross-modal classification framework for handling concept drift that integrates mature unimodal settings with emerging multimodal scenarios and systematically characterizes how drift manifests under adversarial, label-scarce, and class-imbalanced security conditions. Building on prior multimodal learning observations, we propose a consolidated taxonomy of multimodal drift patterns, including synchronous drift, asynchronous drift, and correlation drift. Beyond synthesizing prior work, we construct benchmarks for drift evolution on structured and visual data and, through comparative experiments, show that improved drift adaptability often comes with an increased risk of catastrophic forgetting. Based on the combined survey and empirical analysis, we identify key limitations of current methods in adversarial robustness, label-efficient adaptation, and cross-modal coordination, and outline future directions for developing robust, drift-adaptive security systems.
