AB  - The rapid expansion of digital healthcare systems (E-Health) and the increasing volume of sensitive patient data have intensified the demand for secure, scalable, and privacy-preserving access control mechanisms. Traditional centralized architectures are often inadequate for addressing key concerns such as data leakage, high transaction latency, and excessive resource overhead. This paper introduces a novel adaptive access control framework that integrates blockchain technology, smart contracts, broadcast encryption, and the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) to safeguard medical data. The framework is structured into four cohesive phases: (1) blockchain-based user registration and issuance of digital certificates; (2) classification of medical data into sensitivity levels and assignment of fine-grained access policies; (3) broadcast encryption for efficient and secure group-based key dissemination; and (4) policy enforcement through smart contracts that manage credential validation, encrypted data access via IPFS, and immutable event logging. Formal security proofs based on Hoare logic validate confidentiality, authorized access, and resilience against data poisoning attacks. Empirical evaluations show that the Proposed Method implemented on Hyperledger Fabric outperforms the best-performing baseline approach (HealthRec-Chain), achieving a 23% increase in throughput, a 17% reduction in communication overhead, and a 48% decrease in latency.
