AB  - The growing integration of artificial intelligence (AI) assistants with the Internet of Things (IoT) infrastructure requires systems that can simultaneously ensure security and handle multidomain tasks. Current solutions operate in isolation; traditional intrusion detection systems lack conversational capabilities, whereas AI chatbots are not equipped with the threat awareness required for security-critical applications. This paper presents the design and implementation of a proof-of-concept IoT Intrusion Detection System (IDS) integrated with a multifunctional agentic AI chatbot, demonstrating the feasibility of unifying security monitoring and the orchestration of productivity tasks within a single agentic architecture. The system utilizes a multi-LLM pipeline, wherein Llama-3-8B is responsible for task decomposition and tool selection, and Mistral Medium conducts goal state evaluation. An XGBoost-based IDS trained on UNSW-NB15 serves as the detection engine, with integration capabilities with Google Calendar, Gmail, and DuckDuckGo Search for action-capable responses. The IDS, developed and primarily validated on the UNSW-NB15 dataset, achieved an accuracy of 95.04%. As an additional comparative benchmark, the same modeling approach was independently evaluated on the NSL-KDD dataset, achieving an accuracy of 92.10%. The evaluation of the system with 50 queries resulted in a 96% task completion rate, with a median response times of 0.9 s for direct commands and 4.8 s for complex multi-step tasks.
