AB  - The nexus of legal, ethical, and philosophical challenges posed by the rapid proliferation of artificial intelligence (AI) across global domains is covered in this chapter. It examines how AI, ranging from large language models to autonomous weapons, reshapes notions of liberty, accountability, and justice. Through a metaanalysis of over a hundred AI ethics frameworks, it highlights the gap between aspirational principles and enforceable practices, emphasizing the risks of “ethics-washing.” Legal dimensions are examined through evolving jurisprudence on hallucination-related liabilities, confidentiality breaches, algorithmic bias, and intellectual property disputes. Comparative governance models, such as the European Union AI Act, the US sectoral approach, and China’s cyber-sovereignty framework, illustrate regulatory diversity and emerging convergence on transparency, human oversight, and risk-tiered compliance. Sector-specific analyses cover healthcare diagnostics, employment automation, predictive policing, education, and environmental sustainability, where the benefits of AI are compared against equity, privacy, and ecological concerns. The chapter also discusses new frontiers of academic misconduct, misinformation, and malicious exploitation of generative AI. It concludes that sustainable AI governance must integrate technical safeguards, virtue-based professional ethics, and inclusive global cooperation with foundations in transparency, accountability, and intergenerational justice.
ER  - 
